Examined rationale behind token return functionality
Good question — let's look at what the return token line inside issueToken is actually for, versus what the cookie is for, because they do two different jobs.

javascript
const issueToken = (res, student) => {
    const payload = { ... }
    const token = JWT.sign(payload, process.env.JWT_KEY, { expiresIn: '7h' });

    res.cookie('access', token, { ... });  // job 1: set the cookie

    return token;  // job 2: hand the token back to whoever called this function
}
The cookie (res.cookie(...)) is what actually keeps the user logged in. It's stored in the browser and sent automatically on every future request, so your auth middleware can read it and know who's making the request. That part is already done the moment res.cookie runs — the return has nothing to do with that.

The return token is separate — it's just so the calling function can also put the token in the JSON response body. Look at how you're using it in both places:

javascript
const loginToken = issueToken(res, verifyStudent)

return res.status(200).json({
    message: `${email} is now verified`,
    data: loginToken   // <-- this is why you needed the return
});
Without return token inside issueToken, loginToken would be undefined, and data: loginToken in your response would just be data: undefined — the cookie would still work fine for auth purposes, but your JSON response wouldn't have the token in it.

Why would you even want the token in the response body if the cookie already handles login? A few possible reasons in practice:

Some frontends (especially mobile apps, or SPAs not using cookies) read the token from the response and store it manually (e.g. in memory or Authorization headers) instead of relying on cookies
It's useful for debugging/testing — you can see the token directly in the API response during development
If you ever support a non-cookie client later, the token is already being returned, so you don't have to change the controller
If you genuinely don't need it in the response — i.e. you're relying purely on the httpOnly cookie for auth, which is actually the more secure pattern — you can simplify and just not return loginToken in the JSON body at all:

javascript
issueToken(res, verifyStudent);  // just sets the cookie, ignore the return value

return res.status(200).json({
    message: `${email} is now verified`
});
Either is valid. The return token inside the helper doesn't hurt anything either way — it's just optional data for the caller to use or ignore. Do you want to keep it for the response body, or drop it and rely on the cookie alone?

