This line is inside verifyOtp. So verifyOtp is the one calling issueToken. When this line runs, JavaScript jumps into the issueToken function, runs everything inside it (build payload, sign token, set cookie), hits return token, and jumps back to this exact line in verifyOtp — dropping the returned value into loginToken.

So to be precise about "who":

The user — sends a request (e.g. submits their OTP via a POST request from the frontend). That's what triggers the whole verifyOtp function to run in the first place.
verifyOtp — is the function that, partway through its own execution, calls issueToken(res, verifyStudent).
issueToken — does its job (sign + set cookie), then hands the token value back to verifyOtp via return token.
verifyOtp — takes that returned value, puts it in loginToken, and finally sends the HTTP response back to the user's browser.
So the user starts the chain by making a request, but the user never "calls" issueToken directly — that's an internal call, one function to another, happening entirely on your server before any response goes back out. The user only ever sees the end result: the JSON response and the cookie arriving in their browser.

