Good question, and this is a real, common bug source — let's separate what lowercase actually does from what it doesn't.

Lowercasing (usually done via .toLowerCase() before saving/comparing) exists specifically to solve the "same value, different casing" problem — but it's normally applied to fields that are supposed to be unique identifiers, like email, not to display fields like username.

Here's the distinction:

For email — lowercasing matters a lot, because email lookups need to treat Sara@gmail.com and sara@gmail.com as the same account. If you don't normalize casing, you risk:

User signs up with Sara@gmail.com (saved exactly as typed)
User logs in later typing sara@gmail.com
Student.findOne({email: 'sara@gmail.com'}) finds nothing, because MongoDB does exact string matching by default — different case, different string
Login fails even though it's genuinely the same person, same email
Fix: lowercase it both at signup (before saving) and at login (before querying):

javascript
// at signup
email: email.toLowerCase()

// at login
const CheckEmail = await Student.findOne({ email: email.toLowerCase() })
Even better — enforce it at the schema level so it's automatic everywhere:

javascript
email: { type: String, required: true, unique: true, lowercase: true, trim: true }
Mongoose then lowercases it on save automatically, so you only need to remember to lowercase the incoming login value before querying.

For username — this is exactly why we just agreed to drop it from login entirely. If it's not being used to look anyone up or verify anything, casing mismatches don't matter, because nothing is comparing it against anything. The database just stores whatever string was typed at signup, and it's shown back to the user for display purposes only. So no — lowercase isn't "just for the database," it's specifically a fix for fields used in lookups/comparisons, and username, in your design, is no longer one of those fields.

So to directly answer: the lowercase problem you're describing is real, but it only bites you on fields you actually match against later — which in your final design is just email. Since you're dropping username as a login credential, you don't need to worry about its casing at all anymore. One less thing to get wrong.

